---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';
import { loadManifest } from '@data/loadSnapshot';
import { formatDate } from '@data/derive';

const manifest = loadManifest();
const { ruleset } = manifest;
---

<BlogPostLayout slug="wow-forever-beta-end-and-launch-talent-planning">
  <p>
    If you are planning a character for WoW Forever, the next few weeks are
    crowded with dates: a stress test, the last day of the beta, a name
    reservation window and the launch itself. We keep a single reference page
    for those dates, <a href="/wow-forever-beta-end/">WoW Forever beta end and
    launch dates</a>, and this post walks through the same timeline from a
    narrower angle. The question here is practical: given those dates, what
    can you usefully do with a talent calculator now, and what should you
    leave until later?
  </p>
  <p>
    Every date below is as published by Blizzard and summarized on that page,
    which links to the original announcements. We are an independent fan site
    and do not set or confirm any of them. They have already moved once, and
    this post reflects what the dates page said on
    {' '}{formatDate('2026-10-20')}. If they change again, the dates page is the
    one we update first.
  </p>

  <h2>The timeline</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data-table">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">Announced WoW Forever dates</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Event</th>
          <th scope="col">Date (as announced)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Beta opens</td>
          <td>September 17, 2026 (PST)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Stress test</td>
          <td>
            October 21: 2:00 p.m. PDT in North America, 19:00 CEST in Europe;
            October 22 in Korea (06:00 KST) and Taiwan (05:00 CST)
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Last full day of beta</td>
          <td>October 21, 2026 (PST)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Early Name Reservation</td>
          <td>October 27 to November 3, 2026 (PST)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Global launch</td>
          <td>November 4, 2026, 3:00 p.m. PST</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What each date means in practice</h2>
  <h3>The stress test</h3>
  <p>
    Blizzard moved the stress test from October 19 to October 21 on October
    16. It is open to anyone, with no subscription, Game Time, invitation or
    beta access required, and Blizzard gives no end time. Two details matter
    to anyone who cares about builds. The beta is taken offline shortly before
    the test starts and is expected back shortly after it ends, so the
    beta client is unavailable for a while. And at the end of the test, every character, name and guild created
    on the stress-test servers is wiped before name reservation opens. A
    stress-test character is therefore a place to try the game, not
    somewhere to leave a build you want to keep.
  </p>

  <h3>The last day of the beta</h3>
  <p>
    Blizzard describes October 21 as the last full day of testing. If you
    are in the beta and have been meaning to check a rank text that looks
    wrong against what the game says, this is among the last chances, and a screenshot
    with the build number is the most useful thing you can send us. The
    <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> says what to include.
  </p>

  <h3>Early Name Reservation</h3>
  <p>
    From October 27 to November 3, owners of any upgrade edition can create
    and customize up to three characters. Names are first come, first
    served. This is the first moment a launch character exists, but it is not
    the moment talents matter: in the ruleset this calculator uses, the first
    point is earned at level {ruleset.pointsStartLevel}. Choosing a class and
    a name is separate from deciding a build, and you do not have to settle
    the build during the reservation window.
  </p>

  <h3>Launch</h3>
  <p>
    The game launches worldwide on November 4 at 3:00 p.m. PST, which is
    23:00 UTC. At launch, access is included with a World of Warcraft
    subscription or Game Time, and upgrade editions are optional. The
    countdown on the dates page is set to that moment.
  </p>

  <h2>What this means for talent planning</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing in the announcements we have summarized says whether talents will
    change between now and launch, and we are not going to guess. What we can
    describe is how the site behaves if they do. The calculator runs on a
    dated data snapshot, currently from beta build
    {' '}{manifest.gameBuild ?? 'unknown'} and published
    {' '}{formatDate(manifest.publishedAt)}. When a newer build's data becomes
    available upstream, we publish a new snapshot and add an entry to the
    <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>. Share links made on the old snapshot
    still open on the new one, with renamed talents keeping their points and
    points in removed talents refunded. A build made today is therefore a
    working draft, not a permanent record.
  </p>
  <p>
    With that in mind, here are three things that are worth doing before
    November 4:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Pick a primary tree and know its cost.</strong> Row gates mean
      the deepest tree decides when its talents become available. The
      <a href="/blog/wow-forever-talent-points-levels-and-row-gates/">explainer on points, levels and row gates</a>
      has the numbers, including the earliest level for each row.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Plan in stages instead of one level-60 build.</strong> Set the
      level box to 20, 40 and 60 and see what each version of the plan
      contains. Saving each stage under its own name on
      <a href="/my-builds/">My Builds</a> makes the order easy to follow
      once you are actually leveling.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Back up what you care about.</strong> Saved builds live in your
      browser only. Export the JSON for the ones you want to keep, and keep a
      share link as a second copy. Clearing site data or switching browsers
      removes saved builds.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="post-callout">
    <h3>What to re-check on launch day</h3>
    <p>
      If a new build is released near launch, open your saved builds or share
      links on the calculator. They are migrated to the current snapshot, and
      a note on the page says what was renamed or refunded. The changelog entry
      for that build lists which classes were affected, so if you play a class
      that is not mentioned, your plan has most likely not changed.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Where to follow the official word</h2>
  <p>
    For dates, purchase options and how to join the test, go to
    Blizzard's own announcements. The dates page links to the ones we used:
    the beta announcement, the pre-purchase announcement and the stress-test
    announcement. For the data behind this site's talents and how to judge
    it, see the post on
    <a href="/blog/where-wow-forever-talent-data-comes-from/">where the talent data comes from</a>.
    And if you simply want to start planning, the
    <a href="/">calculator</a> is free and needs no account.
  </p>
</BlogPostLayout>
